<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Merneptah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1213–1203 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Merneptah was the fourth king of the 19th Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>. In his fifth year he defeated a Libyan invasion, joined by contingents of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a>, at Perire in the western Delta. His victory stele, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah-stele">Merneptah Stele</a>, contains the earliest known mention of Israel.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/merneptah.jpg" alt="Merneptah" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hyspaosines · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramses_and_the_Pharaohs%27_Gold,_London_2026-03-06_-_149_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
